{"tool_name":"ccat","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"ccat","version":"3.0","home":"http://cmb.gis.a-star.edu.sg/ChIPSeq/paperCCAT.htm","license":"unknown","summary":"CCAT is a software package for the analysis of ChIP-seq data with negative control.","identifiers":["biotools:CCAT","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btq128","usegalaxy-eu:peakcalling_ccat"],"build":{"number":6},"requirements":{"build":["make",null],"host":null},"source_url":"http://www.comp.nus.edu.sg/~bioinfo/CCAT3.0/CCAT3.0.tar.gz","test":{"commands":["CCAT --help"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"ccat","identifiers":["biotools:CCAT","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btq128"],"license":"Not available","summary":"ccat is a software package for the analysis of chip-seq data with negative control.","home_url":"http://cmb.gis.a-star.edu.sg/ChIPSeq/paperCCAT.htm","keywords":["Binding Sites","Chromatin Immunoprecipitation","Computational Biology","Computer Simulation","Gene Expression Regulation","Genome","Histones","Models, Statistical","Poisson Distribution","Reproducibility of Results","ChIP-seq","DNA packaging"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"ccat","home":"http://cmb.gis.a-star.edu.sg/ChIPSeq/paperCCAT.htm","license":"Not licensed","summary":"Analysis of ChIP-seq data with negative control.","addition_date":"2017-08-20T15:17:50Z","last_update_date":"2020-01-19T17:47:47Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["3.0"],"collections":["Animal and Crop Genomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:ccat","credit":[{"email":"asflin@ntu.edu.sg","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"sungk@gis.a-star.edu.sg","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"note":"Documentation can be found in the downloadable archive.","type":["Training material"],"url":"http://www.comp.nus.edu.sg/~bioinfo/CCAT3.0/"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["animalandcropgenomics"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Peak detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3215"}]}],"name":"CCAT","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"admin","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btq128","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: ChIP-seq is becoming the main approach to the genome-wide study of protein-DNA interactions and histone modifications. Existing informatics tools perform well to extract strong ChIP-enriched sites. However, two questions remain to be answered: (i) to which extent is a ChIP-seq experiment able to reveal the weak ChIP-enriched sites? (ii) are the weak sites biologically meaningful? To answer these questions, it is necessary to identify the weak ChIP signals from background noise. Results: We propose a linear signal-noise model, in which a noise rate was introduced to represent the fraction of noise in a ChIP library. We developed an iterative algorithm to estimate the noise rate using a control library, and derived a library-swapping strategy for the false discovery rate estimation. These approaches were integrated in a general-purpose framework, named CCAT (Control-based ChIP-seq Analysis Tool), for the significance analysis of ChIP-seq. Applications to H3K4me3 and H3K36me3 datasets showed that CCAT predicted significantly more ChIP-enriched sites that the previous methods did. With the high sensitivity of CCAT prediction, we revealed distinct chromatin features associated to the strong and weak H3K4me3 sites. Availability: http://cmb.gis.a-star.edu.sg/ChIPSeq/tools.htm. Contact: sungk@gis.a-star.edu.sg; asflin@ntu.edu.sg. Supplementary Information:Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online. \u00a9 The Author 2010. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved. For Permissions, please email: journals.permissions@oxfordjournals.org.","authors":[{"name":"Handoko L."},{"name":"Lin F."},{"name":"Sheng J."},{"name":"Sung W.-K."},{"name":"Wei C.-L."},{"name":"Wei X."},{"name":"Xu H."},{"name":"Ye C."}],"cita